{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-143279",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a normal population, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\nthen the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ has the distribution\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(7,\\frac{{14}^{2}}{49}) \\\\  & ∼N(7,4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're required to find $P(6.8<\\overset{―}{X}<7.5).$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(6.8<\\overset{―}{X}<7.5) & =P(\\frac{6.8-7}{\\sqrt{4}}<Z<\\frac{7.5-7}{\\sqrt{4}}) \\\\  & =P(-0.1<Z<0.25) \\\\  & =Φ(0.25)-Φ(-0.1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.25)=0.5987,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand by the symmetry of the normal distribution,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.1) & =1-Φ(0.1) \\\\  & =1-0.5398 \\\\  & =0.4602.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(6.8<\\overset{―}{X}<7.5) & =Φ(0.25)-Φ(-0.1) \\\\  & =0.5987-0.4602 \\\\  & =0.1385.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
